  ---                                                                                                                     
  How Each System Handles the Boundary Problem                                                                            
                                                                                                                          
  The answer is surprisingly simple. All systems except Alpa avoid the problem entirely by enforcing uniform TP across all
   pipeline stages.                                                                                                       
                  
  ---                                                                                                                     
  ColossalAI — Uniform TP, enforced by single global tp_size
                                                                                                                          
  HybridParallelPlugin.__init__ (hybrid_parallel_plugin.py:1000-1048):
                                                                                                                          
  def __init__(   
      self,                                                                                                               
      tp_size: int,    # ← one integer, applied to ALL stages
      pp_size: int,    # ← one integer, number of stages                                                                  
      ...
                                                                                                                          
  There is no per-stage TP configuration. tp_size is a scalar that gets applied to the entire model. When the pipeline P2P
   sends activation from stage N to stage N+1, both stages have identical TP degree so the sharding spec matches.
  pipeline/p2p.py does a raw isend/irecv with zero resharding logic — it works only because both sides hold the same      
  shard.          

  ---
  DeepSpeed — Uniform TP via fixed topology dimensions
                                                      
  topology.py:244-248:
                                                                                                                          
  class PipeModelDataParallelTopology(ProcessTopology):
      def __init__(self, num_pp, num_mp, num_dp):                                                                         
          super().__init__(axes=['pipe', 'data', 'model'],                                                                
                           dims=[num_pp, num_dp, num_mp])
          # num_mp = TP degree — same for all num_pp stages                                                               
                                                                                                                          
  PipeModelDataParallelTopology takes a single num_mp value for the entire topology. Every pipeline stage has the same    
  model-parallel (TP) group size. Layer partitioning (module.py:393) uses method='uniform' — equal number of layers per   
  stage, same TP everywhere. No resharding between stages.                                                                
                  
  ---
  Merak — Uniform TP, applied globally before pipeline split
                                                                                                                          
  Merak applies tensor parallelism via ModuleRebuild (pipeline/module.py:43) to the full model first, using a single
  global MP config. The pipeline graph split (graph_shard/split_graph.py) happens after TP is applied — so every stage    
  inherits the same TP degree by construction. There's no concept of per-stage TP configuration.
                                                                                                                          
  ---             
  Alpa — The Only System That Actually Solves Heterogeneous Boundaries
                                                                                                                          
  Alpa is the exception. Its cross_mesh_resharding.py handles the case where adjacent stages have genuinely different
  sharding specs. The mechanism is more complex than a simple all_gather/split:                                           
                  
  ReshardingTaskSpec (cross_mesh_resharding.py:674) stores:                                                               
  - src_sharding_spec — how the tensor is tiled on stage N's mesh (e.g., sharded along axis 1 across 4 GPUs)
  - dst_sharding_spec — how stage N+1's mesh expects the tensor (e.g., replicated across 2 GPUs)                          
                                                                                                
  dst_tile_to_src_tiles_map (cross_mesh_resharding.py:718) builds a mapping from each destination shard to the set of     
  source shards that contribute to it. For arbitrary src→dst sharding changes, multiple source GPUs may need to send      
  pieces to one destination GPU.                                                                                          
                                                                                                                          
  EagerReshardingTask.do() (cross_mesh_resharding.py:113) executes P2P send/recv between the two meshes for each          
  (src_tile, dst_tile) pair — not a collective but point-to-point transfers, which is more flexible and avoids the
  overhead of full all_gather when only partial data needs to move.                                                       
                  
  This is only possible because Alpa works at the XLA compilation level — the resharding tasks are compiled into the XLA  
  computation graph alongside the forward ops. In PyTorch, you'd have to do this at runtime with explicit NCCL calls.
                                                                                                                          
  ---             
  Summary: The Real Answer
                                                                                                                          
  The "boundary problem" only exists when adjacent stages have different TP degrees.
                                                                                                                          
  ColossalAI  → uniform tp_size (one global int)  → no boundary problem                                                   
  DeepSpeed   → uniform num_mp (topology dim)      → no boundary problem                                                  
  Merak       → uniform global MP config           → no boundary problem                                                  
  Alpa (JAX)  → heterogeneous TP allowed           → solves it with cross_mesh_resharding.py
                                                                                                                          
  These systems support "3D parallelism" in the sense of PP × TP × DP, but they require the same TP degree on every stage.
   This is a constraint, not a feature — it's the simplest way to avoid the boundary problem. The cost is that uneven     
  models (e.g., GPT where the embedding layer scales differently than transformer blocks) can't have their TP degrees     
  independently optimized per stage.

  ---
  Implication for Your Plan
                                                                                                                          
  This means Solution B (uniform TP) is not a "heuristic approximation" — it is exactly what every production 3D system 
  does. It's the correct approach for a first implementation.                                                             
                  
  Solution A (heterogeneous TP with Slice-Gather) goes beyond what any of these systems implement in PyTorch. Galvatron   
  does it but only for contiguous layers within the same device mesh, not across P2P pipeline stage boundaries on
  different meshes. Only Alpa does full cross-mesh heterogeneous resharding, and that's tied to XLA compilation.          
                  
  Solution B is the right starting point, and is already production-grade behavior. 


