  Practical Solution for Your Plan
                                  
  For the Solution A boundary resharding, replace the raw dist.all_gather_into_tensor calls in BoundaryAllGather with
  functional collectives so the ops are torch.compile-compatible:                                                         
   
  import torch.distributed._functional_collectives as funcol                                                              
                                                                                                                          
  class _BoundaryAllGather(torch.autograd.Function):
      @staticmethod                                                                                                       
      def forward(ctx, input, tp_group):                                                                                
          ctx.tp_group = tp_group                                                                                         
          # Use functional collective → capturable by torch.compile
          gathered = funcol.all_gather_tensor(                                                                            
              input.contiguous(),                                                                                         
              gather_dim=-1,          # gather along hidden dim
              group=tp_group,                                                                                             
          )                                                                                                             
          # wait() converts AsyncCollectiveTensor → plain Tensor                                                          
          if isinstance(gathered, funcol.AsyncCollectiveTensor):                                                          
              gathered = gathered.wait()
          return gathered                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                          
      @staticmethod
      def backward(ctx, grad):                                                                                            
          # Split the gradient back — local op, no collective                                                           
          tp_size = dist.get_world_size(ctx.tp_group)                                                                     
          rank = dist.get_rank(ctx.tp_group)
          return grad.chunk(tp_size, dim=-1)[rank].contiguous(), None                                                     
                                                                                                                          
  Then wrap the stage forward in torch.compile:                                                                           
                                                                                                                          
  @torch.compile                                                                                                          
  def stage_forward_with_boundary(x):
      out = stage_module(x)                                                                                               
      if boundary_module is not None:                                                                                     
          out = boundary_module(out)   # boundary_module uses functional collective
      return out                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                        
  torch.compile traces through funcol.all_gather_tensor, captures it as an ATen op in the Inductor graph, and compiles the
   collective alongside the stage compute — replicating what Alpa does at XLA compile time. The boundary resharding is no
  longer a separate Python-level call at runtime; it's fused into the compiled stage kernel.                              
                                                                                                                        
  ---
  Summary
         
  Alpa uses XLA because it was built on JAX, not because XLA is uniquely necessary. The gap it closes — compile-time
  knowledge of sharding specs to generate efficient resharding code — is achievable in PyTorch via                        
  torch.distributed._functional_collectives + torch.compile. The one thing PyTorch's DTensor still refuses (cross device 
  mesh comm) isn't needed for the PP+TP boundary case because you don't need DTensor there at all — you use raw functional
   collectives over a manually-created cross-stage process group, then compile the whole thing. That's a complete and   
  practical solution.